a-probe:\n      build:\n        context: ${OSAKA_ROOT:-../..}\n        dockerfile: ${PROBE_DOCKERFILE:-admin/Dockerfile.probe}\n      environment: OSAKA_ADMIN_TOKEN, SSL_CERT_FILE=/usr/local/share/ca-certificates/osaka-test-ca.crt maybe\n      volumes: - ${CERT_DIR}:/certs:ro\n      depends_on: osaka-server\n      command: [\"--probe\", \"https://osaka-server:8443\"]\n      entrypoint: ${PROBE_ENTRYPOINT:-...}\n\nBut we can't have a build if Dockerfile doesn't exist. Since product not delivered, compose isn't run. run-a10.sh checks for admin/ (and entrypoint) before running. Good.\n\nTrust store injection: The probe container's trust must include temp CA. Standard approach for .NET on Linux: copy CA to /usr/local/share/ca-certificates/ and update-ca-certificates. For .NET on Linux, it uses OpenSSL trust store. If the base image has update-ca-certificates, we can mount cert and run update-ca-certificates in entrypoint. But \"临时 CA 注入 probe 容器信任库\" — compose can mount ca.crt into `/usr/local/share/ca-certificates/osaka-test-ca.crt` and set command to run update-ca-certificates before probe. But entrypoint is uncertain.\n\nAlternative: mount CA at `/certs/ca.crt` and set env `SSL_CERT_FILE=/certs/ca.crt` — OpenSSL (and .NET on Linux) honors SSL_CERT_FILE? .NET on Linux uses OpenSSL, and SSL_CERT_FILE is honored by OpenSSL. Actually .NET 8 on Linux uses OpenSSL certificate store; setting SSL_CERT_FILE should work for OpenSSL-based chain building. But this could be fragile. Better: do both: mount ca into `/usr/local/share/ca-certificates/osaka-test-ca.crt` and run `update-ca-certificates` in a wrapper. Hmm.\n\nLet's keep compose with a `PROBE_ENTRYPOINT` variable default to `[\"/probe-entrypoint.sh\"]`? That script would be in tests dir and mounted? But writer may produce Dockerfile. Task says \"临时 CA 注入 probe 容器信任库\". Let's create a small `probe-entrypoint.sh`? But task only lists four files (gen-certs.sh, docker-compose.yml, run-a10.sh, grep-guard.sh). \"写：\" lists these four. We should probably not add extra file? It says \"只准改：tests/admin/admin-connect/ 下的文件\" and \"写：gen-certs.sh、docker-compose.yml、run-a10.sh、grep-guard.sh\". Could add a README? Minimal—maybe not. But an entrypoint wrapper could be embedded into compose as command with sh -c. E.g.:\n\nservices:\n  osaka-probe:\n    image: ${PROBE_IMAGE:-...}\n    entrypoint: [\"/bin/sh\",\"-c\"]\n    command: [\"update-ca-certificates >/dev/null 2>&1 || true; exec ${PROBE_ENTRYPOINT} --probe https://osaka-server:8443\"]\n\nBut entrypoint var expansion inside command string. Compose JSON array with interpolation works. However using `exec $PROBE_ENTRYPOINT` requires splitting. Could be `exec /app/probe --probe ...`.\n\nWe can define `PROBE_ENTRYPOINT` as `/app/admin-probe`. \"待写手交付后对齐\" comment at top.\n\nHmm, but comments in compose. Use `x-` extension fields for notes.\n\nAlso need server cert injection each case: osaka-server must serve different certs for cases ①/② vs ③ vs ④. For ③, server uses cert signed by untrusted CA but probe trusts only temp CA → reject. For ④, server uses cert signed by trusted CA but SAN=other-host → reject. We need per-case server cert paths. Could use environment vars OSAKA_SERVER_CERT / OSAKA_SERVER_KEY per scenario, set via env file or compose environment. gen-certs.sh generates directories:\n- certs/trusted/ server.crt, server.key (SAN osaka-server) + ca.crt\n- certs/untrusted/ server.crt, server.key (signed by untrusted CA) + ca.crt (probe still trusts only trusted/ca.crt)\n- certs/wronghost/ server.crt, server.key (SAN other-host)\nThen compose server volumes mount `${CERT_DIR}` to /certs and env OSAKA_TLS_CERT=/certs/${SERVER_CERT:-server.crt}, etc. run-a10.sh sets SERVER_CERT_DIR to each scenario and recreates server container, probe token accordingly.\n\nMaybe simpler: compose env `OSAKA_TLS_CERT: /certs/${OSAKA_CERT_SET:-trusted}/server.crt` and key. And mount `${CERTS_DIR}`. run-a10.sh exports OSAKA_CERT_SET=trusted/untrusted/wronghost.\n\nFor case ②, same server cert trusted, but token env wrong for probe. run-a10.sh sets PROBE_TOKEN.\n\nNeed to wait for server ready before probe. In compose, `depends_on` doesn't wait for healthy. Add healthcheck using... The server needs auth; healthcheck could use curl with token? Python image may not have curl. Could use python urllib with `-k`? Verification would fail without trust. Use python with ssl context cafile and hostname. Healthcheck:\n  test: [\"CMD\", \"python\", \"-c\", \"import ssl,urllib.request; ctx=ssl.create_default_context(cafile='/certs/ca.crt'); req=urllib.request.Request('https://osaka-server:8443/v1/health'); req.add_header('Authorization','Bearer '+__import__('os').environ['OSAKA_ADMIN_TOKEN']); urllib.request.urlopen(req, context=ctx, timeout=3)\"]\nBut for untrusted/wronghost cert sets, healthcheck will fail (cert untrusted/mismatch), which is expected. Then depends_on with condition service_healthy would block probe. So don't use healthcheck as a gate for those cases. Instead run-a10.sh can wait from host? But probe is in container; server on docker network. Could use `docker compose run` the probe with retry loop. Or use a separate one-shot \"wait\" built into probe script (retries). Better: run-a10.sh runs `docker compose up -d osaka-server`, then polls server readiness by running a temporary python container on the same network with the CA? That's more complexity. Alternatively probe entrypoint itself can retry connection for a few seconds. Unknown product behavior.\n\nSimplest: compose includes healthcheck for server, but run-a10.sh waits via `docker compose up -d --wait osaka-server` only for case ①? For ③/④ the healthcheck fails due to cert verification, but from server perspective it's still serving. We can make healthcheck skip TLS verification? That contradicts no-skip in product but healthcheck is test infra, not product. It would just check process listening, but task says \"源码 grep 无 ... 放行回调\" only product source. Test infra skip-verify healthcheck might muddy the grep? grep only admin/ windows/ probably. But security-wise, healthcheck with `-k` is just liveness, fine. But our grep guard could scan only admin/ and windows/ product. Hmm. To avoid any appearance of skipping validation, we can healthcheck TCP connect only: python socket connect to osaka-server:8443. That checks the port is open without cert. Good, no TLS skip. Use python:\n  test: [\"CMD\", \"python\", \"-c\", \"import socket; socket.create_connection(('osaka-server',8443),3).close()\"]\nNo cert involved. Then `docker compose up -d --wait osaka-server` works for all cases. Great.\n\nFor probe container: run via `docker compose run --rm osaka-probe` with env. The compose service has entrypoint. Or `docker compose run --rm -e ... osaka-probe`. But compose run returns the command exit code. Good.\n\nHowever, `docker compose run` builds the probe image. The default `command` specified. We need to pass `--probe https://osaka-server:8443` per task. Let's put it in compose command explicitly.\n\nExit code mapping: Define at top of run-a10.sh:\nPROBE_OK=0 (成功显示版本/健康)\nPROBE_REJECTED=1 (拒绝且不显示已连接) default generic.\nPending alignment: maybe 2 auth, 3 tls chain, 4 hostname. We can assert:\n  case ①: exit code == 0 and output maybe includes version/health? Task: \"probe 成功显示版本/健康\". Probe likely prints them. We can grep output for version/health fields. But unknown exact output. Define expected markers at top: e.g. `EXPECT_OK_MARKER` maybe \"version\"/\"health\"/\"已连接\". The admin UI shows \"已连接\" plus version/health. Probe output likely JSON of health/version. To avoid fabricating, put pending variables: `PROBE_OUT_OK_PATTERN` default maybe regex `\"(version|health|已连接)\"`. But \"不要编造产品的函数名：需要产品入口时，在脚本顶部集中定义成常量/待定项\". We can define patterns as待定 with honest defaults and note they may need alignment. Tests of user-visible behavior should inspect exit codes primarily; task says \"按退出码判定四项\". So rely on exit codes. For ① assert 0; ②③④ assert nonzero. Then \"③④ 不得显示已连接\" — check output does not contain the \"已连接\"/connected marker. Define `CONNECTED_MARKER=\"${CONNECTED_MARKER:-已连接}\"` with note align. Also \"不显示已连接\" for ②. Good.\n\nCould also distinguish rejection reasons, but not required by task. Keep simple: nonzero.\n\nWait, \"A10 四项必测：①... → probe 成功显示版本/健康\" — probe output should show. We can assert exit 0 and that output matches `VERSION_PATTERN`/`HEALTH_PATTERN` if printed. Let's define `OK_OUTPUT_PATTERN` default `(version|健康|健康检查|health)` and if output is empty, maybe still pass? Better to require marker. But without product alignment it could false fail. Hmm. In CI when writer delivers, the probe should print something. The task explicitly says \"probe 成功显示版本/健康\". I'll implement: for case 1, exit 0 required; additionally require output to contain at least one of the defined success markers. Markers as constants at top with comment \"待写手交付后对齐\". This is reasonable.\n\nAlso need probe token: correct token and wrong token. Define `OSAKA_ADMIN_TOKEN` env in compose for server and `PROBE_TOKEN` for probe. run-a10.sh uses fake values: `TEST_ADMIN_TOKEN=osaka-test-fake-token-0000` (obvious fake). Wrong token `wrong-token-obviously-fake`.\n\ngen-certs.sh must generate certs. Let's write.\n\nrun-a10.sh flow:\n- set -euo pipefail\n- Header comment with purpose, prerequisites, run command, acceptance IDs (A10, A14).\n- Top constants:\n  ROOT=... (repo root): script dir is tests/admin/admin-connect; root = ../../../.\n  ADMIN_DIR=\"$ROOT/admin\"; SERVER_DIR=\"$ROOT/server\"\n  PROBE_SERVICE=osaka-probe; SERVER_SERVICE=osaka-server\n  PROBE_URL=https://osaka-server:8443\n  PROBE_ENTRYPOINT default? The compose file handles entrypoint.\n  PROBE_OK=0\n  CONNECTED_MARKER=已连接\n  Version/health markers.\n- Check product delivered:\n  if [ ! -d \"$SERVER_DIR\" ] || [ ! -d \"$ADMIN_DIR\" ]; then echo \"待测：模块未交付（server/ 或 admin/ 缺失）\"; exit 0; fi\n  Also check docker availability? If not in CI, print \"待 CI\"? Task: scripts face GitHub Actions, local no JDK/SDK/.NET, no run build; top note \"待 CI\". But local run should output \"待测：模块未交付\" if product missing. Product missing so exits 0. If docker missing but code present, maybe \"待 CI：本机无 docker\"? Hmm environment says GitHub Actions ubuntu-latest + docker. We can check docker and if missing, print \"待 CI：需要 docker\" exit 0? That could mask failures in CI if docker missing. Better exit nonzero in CI? The instruction says \"产品代码未交付时：测试必须清楚输出'待测：模块未交付'并以 pytest skip（或脚本退出码 0 + 该提示）结束\". Only product missing gets exit 0. If docker missing when delivered, exit non-zero with clear error. But local run now only product missing path matters.\n- temp dir via mktemp -d, trap cleanup (docker compose down -v --remove-orphans; rm -rf tmp).\n- gen-certs.sh \"$tmp/certs\"\n- export CERTS_DIR=$tmp/certs\n- docker compose -f docker-compose.yml build? `up -d --wait osaka-server` builds probe image too maybe. Use `docker compose up -d --build --wait osaka-server`; then for each scenario, set env and `docker compose up -d --force-recreate --wait osaka-server`, then `docker compose run --rm -e PROBE_TOKEN=... osaka-probe`. Actually compose run service command already. Need network: compose creates network; `docker compose run` joins it. Good.\n- For each case, capture output and exit code. Since set -e, use `if output=$(docker compose ... 2>&1); then rc=0; else rc=$?; fi`.\n- Case 1 expects rc 0 and marker. Cases 2-4 expect rc != 0 and no CONNECTED_MARKER.\n- Print per case PASS/FAIL and summary. Exit 1 if any fail.\n- Need to wait for server readiness in each scenario. `docker compose up -d --wait` uses healthcheck (TCP). Good.\n- Case 2 uses trusted cert, wrong token → probe rejects.\n- Case 3 untrusted cert. But server cert env: OSAKA_CERT_SET=untrusted. Probe trusts only trusted CA. Server starts with untrusted cert; healthcheck TCP works. Probe rejects.\n- Case 4 wronghost cert set. Probe rejects due hostname mismatch.\n\nImportant: The server container env: `OSAKA_ADMIN_TOKEN: ${OSAKA_ADMIN_TOKEN:?}` required. For compose up to not warn. We pass same correct token for all cases; wrong token only to probe in case 2.\n\nCert dirs from gen-certs.sh: trusted/{ca.crt,server.crt,server.key}, untrusted/{ca.crt,server.crt,server.key}, wronghost/{ca.crt,server.crt,server.key}. Probe mounts ${CERTS_DIR}/trusted/ca.crt to trust store path. If we mount a single file, and set OSAKA_CA_FILE? For .NET, use SSL_CERT_FILE=/certs/trusted/ca.crt maybe. Let's include environment in probe:\n  SSL_CERT_FILE: /certs/ca.crt\n  Also mount trusted ca to /usr/local/share/ca-certificates/osaka-test-ca.crt. But container may not allow update-ca-certificates at run time without root; likely root. We can't control writer's Dockerfile. Hmm.\n\nMaybe the writer's probe Dockerfile should handle CA. But we are test, providing compose. Task explicitly says compose injects temp CA into probe trust store. Let's do via volume:\n  volumes:\n    - ${CERTS_DIR}/trusted/ca.crt:/usr/local/share/ca-certificates/osaka-test-ca.crt:ro\nThen entrypoint must run update-ca-certificates. We can set compose `entrypoint` to a command that runs update-ca-certificates then executes probe. But if image's entrypoint is the probe itself, overriding loses it. Define `PROBE_BIN` var, default `/app/admin` maybe. Hmm.\n\nAlternative: use `SSL_CERT_FILE` and `SSL_CERT_DIR` env only; .NET 8 on Linux: .NET uses OpenSSL and respects SSL_CERT_FILE / SSL_CERT_DIR (I believe .NET's OpenSSL cert store respects these). Actually .NET on Linux: `X509Chain` uses OpenSSL; environment variables SSL_CERT_FILE and SSL_CERT_DIR are honored by OpenSSL when using default verify paths. .NET's `OpenSslCertificateStore` uses X509_STORE_set_default_paths, which reads those env vars. So it should work. But to be robust, add both env and the mounted cert with an entrypoint wrapper.\n\nMaybe we can define probe service with:\n    entrypoint: [\"/bin/sh\", \"-c\"]\n    command:\n      - |\n        if command -v update-ca-certificates >/dev/null 2>&1; then update-ca-certificates >/dev/null 2>&1 || true; fi\n        exec \"${PROBE_BIN}\" --probe \"${PROBE_URL}\"\nHowever `PROBE_BIN` env interpolation inside command string is tricky: use $$ to defer to container shell: `exec \"$$PROBE_BIN\" --probe \"$$PROBE_URL\"`. Set environment PROBE_BIN=/app/probe, PROBE_URL=https://osaka-server:8443. Default PROBE_BIN with variable substitution `${PROBE_BIN:-/app/probe}` but that's host interpolation into environment, works: PROBE_BIN: ${PROBE_BIN:-/app/probe}. Good. The entrypoint `/bin/sh -c` requires the image to have /bin/sh — dotnet runtime image does. Fine.\n\nBut wait, the probe is a .NET app; its Dockerfile/image must include it. The compose `image: ${PROBE_IMAGE:-osaka-admin-probe:local}` plus `build:` context/dockerfile variables. If writer's Dockerfile path differs, default `admin/Dockerfile.probe`. \"待写手交付后对齐\" note. If image build fails, CI fails, which is correct once code delivered.\n\nServer service: image python:3.12-slim, mount server. But server module could need dependencies; requirements may exist. The CI code uses pip install -r requirements.txt. For container, command could be `sh -c 'pip install -r requirements.txt && exec python -m server'` but that needs network at runtime (CI has network to pull, pip install from PyPI — forbidden to us locally but CI okay? \"不联网\" applies to us/scripts? Rule 7: 禁止联网. Test scripts running in CI might pip install. However product server must use stdlib only per architecture (Python standard library). Board summary: standard library. So no pip. Use `python:3.12-slim`, command `${SERVER_CMD:-python -m server}`. Environment OSAKA_TLS_CERT=/certs/${OSAKA_CERT_SET}/server.crt etc. working_dir /workspace, volume ${ROOT}/server:/workspace/server:ro. Actually `python -m server` requires server package in /workspace. Mount repo server dir to /workspace/server. Set PYTHONPATH=/workspace maybe. cwd /workspace.\n\nCould instead build a Dockerfile for server; but no Dockerfile. Using image+volume is minimal and no product file assumptions beyond server package. Fine.\n\nBut no host networking; using compose default network. Good. \"不走本机回环\" satisfied; no ports published at all.\n\nNow grep-guard.sh:\n- Purpose: A14 stack check + A10 source grep no ServerCertificateCustomValidationCallback (and returns true permissive callbacks).\n- Should it check product dirs if missing? \"产品代码未交付时输出待测...退出码 0\". If admin/ and windows/ and server/ etc. missing, print \"待测：模块未交付\" and exit 0.\n- If delivered: \n  - A14: windows/ and admin/ only .csproj/.cs/.xaml (and maybe .sln? acceptance says only *.csproj/*.cs/*.xaml. But .sln would be needed to build... A14 says \"只允许 *.csproj/*.cs/*.xaml\". Hmm .sln not listed. CI uses *.sln. This is a conflict. Maybe A14 permits solution file? Acceptance literally: `windows/`、`admin/` 下只允许 `*.csproj`/`*.cs`/`*.xaml`. But CI's windows job looks for *.sln. Strictly A14 would reject .sln. The task says \"另外 A14、源码 grep 无 ServerCertificateCustomValidationCallback / 返回 true 的放行回调.\" We implement A14 check per acceptance, but maybe allow .sln? Better stick to acceptance? Yet .NET projects commonly have .sln. Hmm, the writer may create .sln. If grep-guard fails on .sln, that's a false failure. Acceptance explicitly lists allowed extensions, not .sln, but a build needs it? Actually can build .csproj directly, no .sln required. CI's windows job glob includes .csproj. So .sln isn't necessary. But it might be delivered. The acceptance is authoritative; however is this grep-guard our test responsibility? Yes \"另外 A14\". I could allow `.sln` and note? Ponytail: follow acceptance literally. But a false failure would be bad. Let me think: A14 wording in acceptance is explicit: only *.csproj/*.cs/*.xaml. But also \"docs/architecture.md 技术栈一节与此一致\". I'd implement allowed extensions exactly, plus maybe `.sln` is a build file, not production code; the intent is language restriction. The strict list is what acceptance says. Yet .sln is XML not a language. The risk of false failure. Hmm. Since this is a test script, I can allow `.sln`, `.props`, `.targets`? That deviates. Minimal: follow acceptance strict list, but in the comment mention .sln is not allowed by A14 as written; \"待组长确认\" maybe. However tests should reflect acceptance. I'll implement allowed as per acceptance: `*.csproj`, `*.cs`, `*.xaml`. But also include `.sln`? Let me re-read: \"CI 脚本：`windows/`、`admin/` 下只允许 `*.csproj`/`*.cs`/`*.xaml`；`android/` 下只允许 Kotlin/Gradle(KTS)；`server/` 下只允许 Python；违例即失败。\" Yes. I'll stick exactly. But then the writer would need no .sln. If they add one, test fails, which is per acceptance. Fine.\n\n  - android/: allow .kt, .kts, .gradle? Acceptance: \"只允许 Kotlin/Gradle(KTS)\". Typical Android has .xml resources (layouts), .properties, gradle wrapper jar/scripts. Strictly \"Kotlin/Gradle(KTS)\" would reject XML resources needed for manifest. That's too strict and would fail any real Android app. Hmm. The acceptance is simplified. Maybe A14's intent is production code language. The task only asks us to check A14; \"源码 grep 无 ...\". Maybe we should implement A14 as a production-code extension check: find files with programming-language extensions and ensure allowed. Allow resources/config. That's more realistic. Define: scan files under dirs, skip non-source resources; check source extensions match.\n    - windows/admin allowed source extensions: .cs, .xaml, .csproj; also .sln? skip it as build config? I'd include .sln. Hmm.\n    - android allowed: .kt, .kts, .gradle, .java? No Java per A14. But Gradle wrapper .jar binary, .properties, .xml, .png allowed as resources/config. The check should reject other languages like .py, .js, .ts, .c, .cpp, .rs, .go, .swift, .java? Java is not Kotlin, so reject .java. The statement \"只允许 Kotlin/Gradle(KTS)\" suggests .java forbidden.\n    - server allowed: .py; also requirements.txt? no requirements since stdlib. But maybe .txt config. Source code check .py only.\n  - A10 grep: search admin/ (and windows/? The task says \"源码 grep 无 ServerCertificateCustomValidationCallback / 返回 true 的放行回调\". A10 is about admin; A14 mentions all. The acceptance: \"代码中不得出现跳过证书校验的回调\". Client-side all .NET and Android. Search windows/, admin/, android/ for `ServerCertificateCustomValidationCallback`, and patterns returning true. Also Android custom TrustManager? The task specifically mentions the callback. We can grep for the literal in windows/ admin/ android/ (Kotlin wouldn't have it, but harmless). Also grep `ServerCertificateValidationCallback` (ServicePointManager). And permissive pattern `ServerCertificateCustomValidationCallback` with `=> true` on same or next lines. Better: flag any occurrence of ServerCertificateCustomValidationCallback at all, since A10 says code must not contain skip callback. Even a callback doing proper validation is \"自定义回调\" but acceptance says \"不得出现跳过证书校验的回调\" and task says \"无 ServerCertificateCustomValidationCallback / 返回 true 的放行回调\" — flag all occurrences of ServerCertificateCustomValidationCallback. Also flag `HttpClientHandler.*ServerCertificateCustomValidationCallback` etc. And `ServicePointManager.ServerCertificateValidationCallback`. Also Android `X509TrustManager` custom checkClientTrusted empty? Task doesn't demand; but A10 \"跳过证书校验的回调\" could include. Keep to task: ServerCertificateCustomValidationCallback / returning true. Add `checkServerTrusted`? Ponytail: don't overbuild. Just the named callback and ServicePointManager variant, plus `=> true` patterns. Actually scanning all product code for callback name is the key.\n\n- Output: print PASS lines or fail list; exit 1 on violation. If no product code at all → \"待测：模块未交付\" exit 0.\n- Should be shell only, no deps.\n\nNow gen-certs.sh details:\n- Header comment (purpose, prereq, run command, acceptance).\n- Usage: gen-certs.sh <output-dir>\n- Use `set -euo pipefail`, umask 077.\n-