ack. But A10 acceptance: \"代码中不得出现跳过证书校验的回调\". For Android, a custom X509TrustManager with empty checkServerTrusted is a skip. But admin is .NET; A10 is admin. The task specifically: \"源码 grep 无 ServerCertificateCustomValidationCallback / 返回 true 的放行回调\". So scan .NET code. Could restrict to windows/ admin/ for callbacks; but scanning all is harmless.\n\nAlso direct `HttpClientHandler` with `DangerousAcceptAnyServerCertificateValidator`. Add pattern:\n`DangerousAcceptAnyServerCertificateValidator` (from .NET). This is a known bypass. Also `RemoteCertificateValidationCallback`. But don't overbuild. Add patterns list:\n- ServerCertificateCustomValidationCallback\n- ServerCertificateValidationCallback\n- DangerousAcceptAnyServerCertificateValidator\nThese are clearly prohibited. The first two alone cover.\n\nWe should exclude test files? If admin has unit tests with a callback to test rejection, grep would flag. Task says \"源码 grep 无 ServerCertificateCustomValidationCallback\" without exception. Acceptance says code must not contain skip callback. Tests might legitimately use one to simulate. But strict grep is what's asked. Keep strict.\n\nOutput: for each hit print; set viol=1. At end if viol, \"grep-guard 失败\"; exit 1. Else \"grep-guard 通过\". Include A14 results.\n\nAlso docs/architecture.md tech stack consistency? A14 verification says \"另查 docs/architecture.md 技术栈一节与此一致\". Could grep docs for the tech stack line. We can add a simple check that architecture.md contains \".NET\", \"Kotlin\", \"Python\" in the 技术栈 section. That's maybe overkill. The task says \"另外 A14\". A14 includes docs consistency. Minimal check: extract the text after \"## 技术栈（A14）\" and assert it mentions C#/.NET, Kotlin, Python. Let's add a `check_architecture` function. But ponytail—only if cheap. It's a few lines:\n```bash\narch=\"$ROOT/docs/architecture.md\"\nif [ -f \"$arch\" ]; then\n  section=$(awk '/^## 技术栈/{flag=1;next}/^## /{flag=0}flag' \"$arch\")\n  for word in '.NET' 'Kotlin' 'Python'; do\n    grep -Fq \"$word\" <<<\"$section\" || { echo \"A14 违例：docs/architecture.md 技术栈缺少 $word\"; viol=1; }\n  done\nfi\n```\nThis is fine. Note docs is not product code but part of A14.\n\ngen-certs self-check: implement.\n\nPotential issue in run-a10.sh: when product missing, the script must not require docker. Check product first, then docker. Good. Local run will print 待测 and exit 0.\n\nLet's also consider that the task says \"脚本面向 GitHub Actions，本机不装 JDK/SDK/.NET、不跑构建；脚本顶部标注'待 CI'.\" So add header line \"运行环境：GitHub Actions ubuntu-latest + docker（待 CI；本机不装 .NET/JDK，不跑构建）\". Good.\n\nShould run-a10.sh call docker compose build? Yes after delivery. `docker compose -p ... -f ... build` builds both services. For server, image python:3.12-slim (no build section) so build just pulls? `compose build` skips image-only services. Probe builds. Use `docker compose build \"$PROBE_SERVICE\"`. Then up server.\n\nCompose project name: `-p \"$COMPOSE_PROJECT\"` in all invocations.\n\n`docker compose run --rm` may use a different project name unless -p. Use -p.\n\nAlso `docker compose run` with `depends_on condition: service_healthy` requires server up; it is.\n\nOne more: `docker compose run` might not use the service's `command`? It does. It overrides entrypoint? No, uses defined entrypoint/command unless overridden. Good.\n\nBut `docker compose run` without `-T` allocates TTY; in CI non-TTY might warn/fail. Add `-T`. `docker compose run --rm -T \"$PROBE_SERVICE\"`. Good. Note: -T is for run.\n\nBuild: `docker compose build` may need network to pull base image in CI. Fine.\n\nNow the compose healthcheck for `docker compose run` dependency condition: When server is up healthy, run proceeds.\n\nCase ③/④ server cert change: `up -d --force-recreate --wait` waits for healthy. Since TCP healthcheck, fine. But it might take time; retries 30*2s=60s. Good.\n\nAnother subtlety: In case 3 the server cert is untrusted, but the server still starts (no client auth). Yes.\n\nCase 4 wronghost cert: server starts, SAN mismatch. Probe rejects.\n\nNow the CA trust mount in compose is always trusted/ca.crt, regardless of cert set. For case 3 the server cert is untrusted, probe still trusts only trusted CA → reject. For case 4 server cert is signed by trusted CA but SAN wrong → hostname mismatch. Correct.\n\nBut wait: in case 4, the probe's trust store has trusted/ca.crt which signed wronghost/server.crt. Chain valid, hostname invalid. Correct.\n\nIn case 3, untrusted CA signs server cert. Probe trusts trusted CA only. Chain invalid. Correct.\n\nNow gen-certs.sh output layout: I said `ca/ca.crt`, `trusted/server.*`, `untrusted/server.*`, `wronghost/server.*`. Compose mounts `${CERTS_DIR}/trusted/ca.crt` for the probe. So put a copy of CA at trusted/ca.crt. gen-certs creates:\n- ca/ca.crt, ca/ca.key\n- trusted/ca.crt (copy), trusted/server.crt/key\n- untrusted/ca.crt, ca.key, server.crt/key\n- wronghost/ca.crt (copy of trusted CA), server.crt/key\nMakes each scenario self-contained. Good.\n\nImplementation with functions. Let me write gen-certs:\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# 用途：为 A10 端到端在运行时生成临时 CA 与服务器证书（可信/SAN=osaka-server、\n#       非受信 CA 签发、SAN 不含 osaka-server 三套），写入调用方给的临时目录。\n#       私钥只在运行时生成，不入库；调用方负责清理目录。\n# 前置环境：openssl 3.x（GitHub Actions ubuntu-latest 自带）。\n# 运行命令：tests/admin/admin-connect/gen-certs.sh <临时输出目录>（由 run-a10.sh 调用）\n# 覆盖 acceptance：A10（①可信证书、③不可信 CA、④主机名不匹配）。\nset -euo pipefail\numask 077\nOUT=${1:?用法: gen-certs.sh <输出目录>}\ncommand -v openssl >/dev/null || { echo \"需要 openssl\" >&2; exit 2; }\nmkdir -p \"$OUT\"/{ca,trusted,untrusted,wronghost}\nSUBJ_PREFIX=\"/CN=Osaka A10 Test\"\n\nmake_ca() { # $1 dir, $2 cn\n  openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:2048 -nodes -days 1 \\\n    -keyout \"$1/ca.key\" -out \"$1/ca.crt\" \\\n    -subj \"/CN=$2-$(date +%s%N)\" \\\n    -addext \"basicConstraints=critical,CA:TRUE\" \\\n    -addext \"keyUsage=critical,keyCertSign,cRLSign\" >/dev/null 2>&1\n}\n\nissue() { # $1 ca dir, $2 out dir, $3 CN, $4 SAN\n  local csr=\"$2/request.csr\"\n  openssl req -new -newkey rsa:2048 -nodes \\\n    -keyout \"$2/server.key\" -out \"$csr\" -subj \"/CN=$3\" >/dev/null 2>&1\n  printf 'subjectAltName=%s\\nbasicConstraints=critical,CA:FALSE\\nkeyUsage=critical,digitalSignature,keyEncipherment\\nextendedKeyUsage=serverAuth\\n' \"$4\" > \"$2/ext.cnf\"\n  openssl x509 -req -in \"$csr\" -days 1 \\\n    -CA \"$1/ca.crt\" -CAkey \"$1/ca.key\" -CAcreateserial \\\n    -out \"$2/server.crt\" -extfile \"$2/ext.cnf\" >/dev/null 2>&1\n  rm -f \"$csr\" \"$2/ext.cnf\"\n}\n\nmake_ca \"$OUT/ca\" \"trusted\"\nmake_ca \"$OUT/untrusted\" \"untrusted\"\ncp \"$OUT/ca/ca.crt\" \"$OUT/trusted/ca.crt\"\ncp \"$OUT/ca/ca.crt\" \"$OUT/wronghost/ca.crt\"\ncp \"$OUT/untrusted/ca.crt\" \"$OUT/untrusted/ca.crt\" # no-op\nissue \"$OUT/ca\" \"$OUT/trusted\" \"osaka-server\" \"DNS:osaka-server\"\nissue \"$OUT/untrusted\" \"$OUT/untrusted\" \"osaka-server\" \"DNS:osaka-server\"\nissue \"$OUT/ca\" \"$OUT/wronghost\" \"other-host\" \"DNS:other-host\"\n```\nWait: `make_ca \"$OUT/untrusted\"` creates untrusted/ca.key and ca.crt in same dir as untrusted/server.*. Fine. `issue \"$OUT/untrusted\" \"$OUT/untrusted\"` writes server.key/crt. Fine. But CA dir and output dir same, and `issue` writes ext.cnf/request.csr; okay.\nHowever `-CAcreateserial` creates `ca.srl` in CWD or CA dir? It creates the serial file next to CA cert? Actually the default serial filename is based on the CA cert filename with .srl extension in the current directory? OpenSSL x509: \"-CAserial filename\" default: the CA certificate filename with .srl suffix. I think it's in the current working directory unless path given? Let me recall: `openssl x509 -req -CA ca.crt -CAcreateserial` creates `ca.srl` in the current directory. Hmm, that would write to whatever cwd (repo root when run). That violates \"不入库\" potentially leaving ca.srl in cwd. We must avoid. Use `-CAserial \"$1/ca.srl\"` explicitly? With -CAcreateserial plus -CAserial path: yes, specify `-CAserial \"$1/ca.srl\" -CAcreateserial`. Then it writes into temp dir. Let's do that. For the second issue with same CA, the serial file exists; using -CAcreateserial with existing file—still okay? To be safe, use `-CAserial \"$1/ca.srl\"` and `-CAcreateserial` only if not exists:\n```bash\nlocal serial_args=(-CAserial \"$1/ca.srl\")\n[ -f \"$1/ca.srl\" ] || serial_args+=(-CAcreateserial)\nopenssl x509 -req ... \"${serial_args[@]}\"\n```\nFor each CA dir, first call creates, second uses. Good.\n\nAlso self-check:\n```bash\nverify_ok() { openssl verify -CAfile \"$1\" \"$2\" >/dev/null 2>&1; }\ncheckhost_ok() { openssl x509 -in \"$1\" -noout -checkhost \"$2\" >/dev/null 2>&1; }\n\nverify_ok \"$OUT/trusted/ca.crt\" \"$OUT/trusted/server.crt\" || die\ncheckhost_ok \"$OUT/trusted/server.crt\" osaka-server || die\n! verify_ok \"$OUT/trusted/ca.crt\" \"$OUT/untrusted/server.crt\" || die  # 预期失败\n! checkhost_ok \"$OUT/wronghost/server.crt\" osaka-server || die\nverify_ok \"$OUT/wronghost/ca.crt\" \"$OUT/wronghost/server.crt\" || die  # 链可信但主机名不匹配\n```\nNote `set -e` with `! verify_ok ... || die`: if verify_ok fails, `!` turns success, `|| die` not run. Good.\n`openssl x509 -checkhost` exists in OpenSSL 1.1.0+. yes.\nFor wronghost, checkhost osaka-server should fail (nonzero) → `!` success.\nAlso check SAN of trusted server contains exactly DNS:osaka-server. -checkhost covers.\n\nDie function:\n```bash\ndie() { echo \"gen-certs 失败：$*\" >&2; exit 1; }\n```\n\nPrint summary:\n```bash\necho \"临时证书已生成：$OUT（trusted/untrusted/wronghost）\"\n```\n\nNow compose. Let me think about `.env` variables. Compose will warn if unset vars with defaults. Use defaults:\n- OSAKA_ROOT default? Compose resolves relative paths relative to compose file directory, not root. `context: ${OSAKA_ROOT:-../../..}` — compose file is at tests/admin/admin-connect, so ../../.. = repo root. But for build context with a relative path containing `..`, allowed. For volumes, `${OSAKA_ROOT:-../../..}/server` — relative paths in volumes are resolved relative to compose file. Good. run-a10 exports OSAKA_ROOT absolute anyway.\n- PROBE_DOCKERFILE default admin/Dockerfile.probe (relative to context). Good.\n- CERTS_DIR required; run-a10 exports absolute.\n- OSAKA_ADMIN_TOKEN required for server; PROBE_TOKEN required for probe.\n- OSAKA_CERT_SET default trusted.\n- PROBE_BIN default /app/probe.\n- PROBE_URL default https://osaka-server:8443.\n- SERVER_CMD default python -m server.\n\nCompose `x-` note fields with Chinese. Compose tolerates unknown top-level `x-` fields.\n\nPotential issue: `build.context` default `../../..` from compose file → /home/box/osaka/wt/tests? Wait the repo root is /home/box/osaka/wt/tests (git worktree root). The compose file is at /home/box/osaka/wt/tests/tests/admin/admin-connect. ../../.. from there = /home/box/osaka/wt/tests. Correct.\n\nDockerfile `admin/Dockerfile.probe` under repo root. Good.\n\nBut wait: .gitignore? The repo root has .gitignore ignoring bin/, obj/, etc. The probe Dockerfile would be delivered by writer in admin/. Our compose references it. Good.\n\nServer service command: `python -m server` from /workspace with `server/` mounted. But if server/ has `__main__.py` or `main.py`? Board says \"Python 后台：标准库 http.server + ssl\", entry unknown. Tests/server uses `python -m server`. So align with that: default `python -m server`. Good; note \"待写手交付后对齐\".\n\nProbe: entrypoint shell wrapper. The `update-ca-certificates` writes to /etc/ssl/certs; needs root (default in containers). The mounted cert path is `/usr/local/share/ca-certificates/osaka-test-ca.crt`. Good.\n\nBut for .NET on Linux, does it use the system CA bundle and/or SSL_CERT_FILE? Likely. We set both. Also maybe `SSL_CERT_DIR` should not be set to /certs/trusted because that would make OpenSSL hash-dir scanning; cert filename isn't hashed, so it may ignore. SSL_CERT_FILE is enough. Remove SSL_CERT_DIR to avoid weirdness? OpenSSL uses SSL_CERT_DIR for hashed dirs. Setting it to a