{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"0afcfc23-bf02-407e-ba4d-7b5d3970b414","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"A few issues to consider:\n\n1. gen-certs.sh line 34: `local serial=(-CAserial \"$ca_dir/ca.srl\")` — Bash `local` with array assignment is supported in bash 4+. `local serial=(-CAserial \"$ca_dir/ca.srl\")` is valid? Let me recall: `local arr=(1 2 3)` works in Bash. Yes.\n\n2. openssl `x509 -req` option order: `-CAserial` and `-CAcreateserial` are valid. Fine.\n\n3. `openssl x509 -req -CAcreateserial` with `-CAserial` explicit: fine.\n\n4. The CA cert `-addext basicConstraints=critical,CA:TRUE` — OpenSSL might require `pathlen`? no.\n\n5. The self-check `openssl verify -CAfile \"$OUT/trusted/ca.crt\" \"$OUT/trusted/server.crt\"` may fail due to the CA cert lacking `subjectKeyIdentifier`? That's fine.\n\n6. Hostname check: `openssl x509 -checkhost` prints and exits 0. Good.\n\nPotential problem: For `wronghost`, the CA is the same as trusted, but `issue` uses `$OUT/ca` CA dir; the `ca.srl` exists from the trusted issue. `serial=(-CAserial \"$OUT/ca/ca.srl\")`, and `[ -f ]` true → no `-CAcreateserial`. Fine.\n\nNow, should we generate server certs with `-CAcreateserial` when the serial file doesn't exist; the first call for `$OUT/ca` is trusted. Good.\n\nNow compose issue: `OSAKA_TLS_CERT: /certs/${OSAKA_CERT_SET:-trusted}/server.crt`. For the untrusted scenario, run-a10 sets `OSAKA_CERT_SET=untrusted` on the `up` command. Compose interpolates. Good.\n\nPotential issue: `docker compose build \"$PROBE_SERVICE\"` needs `CERTS_DIR`, `PROBE_TOKEN`, `OSAKA_ADMIN_TOKEN` for interpolation; all exported. Good.\n\nPotential issue: In cleanup, `docker compose down` will interpolate and requires `PROBE_TOKEN`, etc. exported. Yes.\n\nPotential issue: In `run_probe`, the inline `PROBE_TOKEN=\"$1\" docker compose ...` overrides the exported PROBE_TOKEN for that command. Good.\n\nPotential issue: The `docker compose run` one-off container's `entrypoint` is the shell snippet; it expects `update-ca-certificates` and `$PROBE_BIN`. The image built from writer's Dockerfile must have the probe binary at PROBE_BIN. The env var `PROBE_BIN` from compose default. Good.\n\nPotential issue: The `command` list with `|` block and `$$` escaping. Compose interpolates `$$` → `$`, `PROBE_BIN` env remains. Good.\n\nPotential issue: The server `command: [\"sh\", \"-c\", \"exec ${SERVER_CMD:-python -m server}\"]` — Compose interpolation with default containing a space is okay. But if `SERVER_CMD` env is set in the host environment to something, it substitutes. Good.\n\nNow, one major concern: Does compose interpolate `${...}` inside `command` arrays? Yes.\n\nNow, the test infrastructure uses `docker compose` v2. The `--wait` flag on `up` requires v2.17.0+. GitHub Actions ubuntu-latest has v2.20+. Fine.\n\nNow, a design correctness concern for A10 ③: We mount the trusted CA into probe trust. But `SSL_CERT_FILE=/certs/trusted/ca.crt` means OpenSSL trusts only that CA, not the system store. That's good. For the trusted case, server cert signed by that CA and SAN matches → success. For untrusted → fail. For wronghost → chain OK but hostname mismatch → fail. Good.\n\nBut if the .NET probe relies on the system store (after update-ca-certificates) and ignores SSL_CERT_FILE, the system store also contains only the trusted CA from our mount plus the base image's public CAs. The untrusted CA isn't in the system store → fail. wronghost signed by trusted CA → chain OK, hostname mismatch → fail. Good.\n\nIf `update-ca-certificates` is missing and SSL_CERT_FILE works, fine.\n\nNow, one more issue: The probe container may run as a non-root user (dotnet images default to root unless configured). If non-root, update-ca-certificates fails (|| true) and SSL_CERT_FILE still works. Fine.\n\nNow, should we also pass `OSAKA_ADMIN_TOKEN` into the probe? The compose environment maps `OSAKA_ADMIN_TOKEN: ${PROBE_TOKEN:?...}`. Good; the probe reads the env var per requirement.\n\nNow, a potential issue: the task says \"另一容器跑管理端 probe 入口 --probe https://osaka-server:8443\". Our command is `exec \"$PROBE_BIN\" --probe \"$PROBE_URL\"` where PROBE_URL defaults to that. Good. But the task might expect the literal argument in compose. It's effectively there as env default. Fine.\n\nNow, \"临时 CA 注入 probe 容器信任库\" — done via mount + update-ca-certificates + SSL_CERT_FILE.\n\nNow, \"不走本机回环、不用 host 网络\" — no ports, no host network. Good.\n\nNow, A14 grep: The grep for callbacks scans `server/` too. Python code could contain the string in a comment; harmless but strict. Fine.\n\nNow, one potential false positive: `ServerCertificateValidationCallback` appears in our own test scripts? They're under tests/, not scanned. Good.\n\nNow, the `grep-guard.sh` A14 check of docs: `awk '/^## 技术栈/{flag=1;next}/^## /{flag=0}flag'`. The architecture file has `## 技术栈（A14）` followed by content, then `## 目录与模块`. The awk stops at `## `. The `.NET` string appears in the section. \"Kotlin\" and \"Python\" appear. Good.\n\nNow, the A14 check might falsely flag `.xaml.cs`? ext = cs → allowed. Good.\n`AssemblyInfo.cs` allowed.\n`.editorconfig` ext = editorconfig → not source. Good.\n`.gitignore` no ext → not source.\n`Directory.Build.props` ext props → not source. Good.\n`app.manifest` ext manifest → not source. Good.\n`global.json` ext json → not source.\n`NuGet.config` ext config → not source.\nGood.\n\nFor Android:\n- `settings.gradle.kts` ext kts → allowed.\n- `build.gradle.kts` allowed.\n- `gradle.properties` ext properties → not source.\n- `AndroidManifest.xml` ext xml → not source.\n- `gradlew` no ext → not source.\n- `gradle-wrapper.jar` ext jar → not source.\n- `.kt` allowed.\nGood.\n\nFor server:\n- `.py` allowed.\n- `requirements.txt` ext txt → not source.\n- `.json` etc. ignored.\n- A `.sh` script in server/ would be flagged. Is that desirable? A14 says server only Python. A shell script is not production code, but strict. Could be a CI helper. Probably okay. If the writer adds `server/run.sh`, the test fails; perhaps too strict. The acceptance says server/ only Python. Fine.\n\nNow, do we need to check that `windows/` and `admin/` contain no `.sln`? As discussed, ignored. This deviates from literal A14 but is pragmatic. Should I make it stricter? The task says \"另外 A14\". I think the language-based check is defensible and avoids false failures. But the acceptance explicitly enumerates allowed extensions. Hmm. Let me reconsider: A reviewer might expect the test to flag any non-allowed file. But resources in Android/WPF (images, XAML resources are xaml allowed; .png, .ico, .json) can't be excluded by a literal allow-list. So the acceptance's list is clearly shorthand for source files. Our approach is correct.\n\nOne thing: WPF projects commonly include `.resx` resource files. Our check ignores them (not source). Fine.\n\nNow, do we need a README or notes? The task says only reply with file list. No.\n\nNow, the index file. The preamble says \"醒来只读 ... notes/<模块>.md\". We read notes/admin-connect.md. Good. The task says don't modify board.json. We didn't.\n\nNow, can I verify the scripts at all? Bash blocked. Maybe I can use the `Workflow` or `Agent`? No; the same permission system. The Read/Write tools work. I could try `Bash` with a very specific test-run command like `pytest` which the tester settings allow. But there are no Python tests here. `bash -n` was blocked. Maybe the permission mode is \"don't ask\" and the Bash tool requires pre-approval per command pattern; `pytest` might be allowed. But running shell scripts likely not. Let me try one more time with `dangerouslyDisableSandbox: true`? The instructions say use it to override sandbox; but the approval issue may be sandbox-related. The tool description says \"Set this to true to dangerously override sandbox mode\". The user's role is tester with allowed test running; maybe sandbox blocks writes/chmod. But disabling sandbox without authorization is risky. The task explicitly expects scripts to run in CI, not locally (\"本机不装 JDK/SDK/.NET、不跑构建\"). So local execution of the scripts isn't required. The only local check would be syntax; not essential. I'll skip and note the risk in the final sentence? The user said reply only file list + one sentence. I can say the scripts are CI-oriented and were not executed locally because the test CLI blocks Bash (consistent with the existing \"测试 CLI 权限配置阻断\" note). But the instruction says \"一句话说明\" — I can make it one sentence with that caveat. Hmm, but they may not want excuses. Still, honesty about not running is required by the system (\"Report outcomes faithfully\"). I'll say: \"未在本机执行（测试 CLI 未放行 Bash；按任务卡待 CI 验证）\". That's a second sentence though. The user says \"完成后只回复：新建的文件清单 + 一句话说明。\" I can put the caveat in the one sentence. Maybe: \"四个脚本均面向 CI，本机因测试 CLI 未放行 Bash 未执行，产品未交付路径会输出“待测：模块未交付”并以 0 退出。\" That's one sentence.\n\nWait, I should double check whether the `Write` tool created the files with the correct directory: /home/box/osaka/wt/tests/tests/admin/admin-connect. Yes.\n\nOne more important issue: The task says \"只准改：tests/admin/admin-connect/ 下的文件\". We created exactly 4 files there. Good.\n\nNow, one more thought about the `run-a10.sh` exit code semantics for ②③④. If the probe returns 125 (docker error), `rejected` is false → FAIL. Good. But if `docker compose run` returns 1 because the probe app returns 1 on rejection, pass. If the probe app returns 2 for auth and 3 for TLS, pass. Good.\n\nPotential problem: The probe may return 0 even on rejection if the product's probe mode is not implemented; then ②/③/④ fail. Correct.\n\nPotential problem: The probe prints \"已连接\" on success but also maybe on failure? Unlikely.\n\nNow, the \"OK_MARKER_REGEX\" default includes `version|health|健康|版本`. If the probe prints `{\"service\":\"osaka-server\",\"version\":\"0.1.0\",\"protocol\":\"admin.v1\"}` → \"version\" matches. If it prints health JSON `{\"status\":\"ok\",\"routes\":[...]}`, \"health\" doesn't appear in the body, but the probe might print a label. To be safer, include `status` and `routes`? The task says \"显示版本/健康\". The probe might print the health response only (`status ok`, `routes`). Then our regex would fail case ①. Hmm. We should make the success marker broader: `(version|health|status|routes|健康|版本|已连接)`. But then it could match a failure message containing \"status\"? Unlikely. Better to define the marker as any of the response indicators. The task says success should show version/health. A probe that prints a health JSON body would contain `\"status\"`. Let's include `status|routes`. Also the version body contains `service`, `version`, `protocol`. Include `service|protocol`. A broad regex `(version|health|status|routes|service|protocol|健康|版本|已连接)` is robust. But if the probe prints an error body containing `\"code\"` only, no match. Good.\n\nAlternatively, don't require a marker at all; just exit 0. But the task explicitly says \"成功显示版本/健康\". Requiring the marker is good. Make it broad.\n\nLet me update `OK_MARKER_REGEX` default to `(version|health|status|routes|service|protocol|健康|版本|已连接)`. This reduces false failures.\n\nAnother thought: The probe might output only \"OK\" or \"已连接\". `已连接` included. Good.\n\nLet me update.\n\nAlso, `CONNECTED_MARKER` default \"已连接\"; if the UI uses \"连接成功\" instead, alignment needed. The top comment says待对齐. Fine.\n\nNow, another potential issue: The task says \"①临时 CA 签发、SAN=osaka-server 的证书 + 正确令牌 → probe 成功显示版本/健康\". Our case ① uses `trusted` cert and correct token. Good.\n\n\"③证书由另一个非受信 CA 签发 → 拒绝\" — untrusted. Good.\n\"④证书 SAN 不含 osaka-server（如 SAN=other-host）→ 拒绝\" — wronghost. Good.\n\n\"③④ 不得显示已连接\" — checked.\n\n\"A14、源码 grep 无 ServerCertificateCustomValidationCallback / 返回 true 的放行回调\" — grep-guard.\n\nNow, should `grep-guard.sh` check that the probe is .NET (A14)? A14 says admin/ only C#/.NET. Our check allows cs/csproj/xaml. Good.\n\nNow, one thing: `grep-guard.sh` prints \"待测：模块未交付\" only if all four dirs are absent. In the current repo, windows/, admin/, android/, server/ are all absent → prints the message and exit 0. Good.\n\nLet me update the regex.","signature":"0afcfc23-bf02-407e-ba4d-7b5d3970b414"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2305,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":68736,"output_tokens":0,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"85b5f7a4-f572-472d-8399-89c76017bd29","uuid":"f3d17f23-5b08-4291-927b-99536fd450ef","timestamp":"2026-11-02T05:06:01.973Z","thinking_duration_ms":18323,"thinking_display":"updates"}
